:::agent How to work through this list
- List the open tasks with `smd tasks tasks.smd` (or the MCP `tasks` tool): overdue first, then by priority. Take the first one that isn't blocked.
- Read the requirement a task names (R1) in the spec, with `smd agent spec.smd --section requirements`, before you start.
- When a task is done and its checks pass (the tests, `smd validate`), check it off: change `- [ ]` to `- [x]` on its line, or call `check_task` with the file and line when the MCP server runs with `--allow-write`. Never check off a task whose checks fail.
- Found more work? Add a task to the right phase, with the requirement, a priority and an owner. Don't do unlisted work silently.
:::

## Phase 1: Foundation

- [ ] Set up the smallest end-to-end slice (R1) :priority[P0] @owner :due[{{today}}]
- [ ] Test AC1 (R1) :priority[P0] @owner :due[{{today}}]

## Phase 2: Complete

- [ ] Implement the next requirement (R2) :priority[P1] @owner
- [ ] Handle the failure cases and test AC2 (R2) :priority[P1] @owner

## Phase 3: Release

- [ ] Roll out behind a flag, then to everyone :priority[P1] @owner
- [ ] Update the docs and the changelog :priority[P2] @owner

## Done when

- Every P0 and P1 task is checked off.
- Every acceptance criterion in the spec passes.
- `smd validate` reports no errors in the spec, the plan and this list.
